---
/**
 * /concepcion-safety/ — EN city editorial page (non-FAQ)
 *
 * Loads Concepción (CUT 8101) via loadCommune. DataCallouts for rate vs national + trend.
 * WebPage JSON-LD. esPath=/es/seguridad-concepcion/ (EDIT-02 verbatim).
 *
 * CRITICAL: uses loadNationalAverage() (MEAN), latestCompleteYearRate() — never SUM.
 */
import EditorialLayout from '../layouts/EditorialLayout.astro';
import DataCallout from '../components/DataCallout.astro';
import { loadCommune, loadNationalAverage, latestCompleteYearRate } from '../lib/data.ts';

const concepcion = loadCommune('8101');
const year = concepcion.latestCompleteYear;
const rate = latestCompleteYearRate(concepcion);
const nationalAvg = loadNationalAverage();
const vsNationalPct = nationalAvg > 0
  ? Math.round(((rate - nationalAvg) / nationalAvg) * 100)
  : 0;
const trendLabel =
  concepcion.trend === 'up' ? 'rising' :
  concepcion.trend === 'down' ? 'falling' :
  'stable';

const jsonLd = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "WebPage",
  "name": "Concepción Safety — CEAD Crime Data",
  "description": "Reported crime statistics for Concepción commune from CEAD official data.",
  "about": {
    "@type": "Dataset",
    "name": "CEAD Crime Statistics — Concepción",
    "publisher": {
      "@type": "GovernmentOrganization",
      "name": "Centro de Estudios y Análisis del Delito (CEAD)",
    },
  },
};
---

<EditorialLayout
  lang="en"
  title="Concepción Safety — Crime Statistics and Data Overview — Chile Safety Map"
  description="Official CEAD crime statistics for Concepción commune. Reported rates, trend direction, national comparison, and practical context for visitors and residents."
  enPath="/concepcion-safety/"
  esPath="/es/seguridad-concepcion/"
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <section>
    <h1>Concepción Safety: CEAD Crime Data for Chile's Second City</h1>
    <p>
      Concepción is Chile's second largest urban area and the capital of the Biobío Region.
      A major university city, industrial centre, and transport hub, it has a large student
      population and a diverse economic base. This page reports what CEAD official data shows
      for the commune of Concepción for {year}.
    </p>

    <DataCallout
      value={`${Math.round(rate).toLocaleString('en-US')} per 100k`}
      label={`Concepción commune reported crime rate — CEAD ${year}`}
      source="Source: CEAD (Centro de Estudios y Análisis del Delito)"
      locale="en"
    />

    <DataCallout
      value={`${Math.round(nationalAvg).toLocaleString('en-US')} per 100k`}
      label={`National average reported crime rate — CEAD ${year}`}
      source="Source: CEAD — unweighted mean of non-low-population communes"
      locale="en"
    />
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>How Concepción Compares with the National Average</h2>
    <p>
      According to CEAD data for {year}, Concepción's reported crime rate of
      {Math.round(rate).toLocaleString('en-US')} per 100,000 inhabitants is approximately
      {Math.abs(vsNationalPct)} % {vsNationalPct >= 0 ? 'above' : 'below'} the national
      mean of {Math.round(nationalAvg).toLocaleString('en-US')} per 100,000. The current
      trend is {trendLabel} according to the CEAD series for recent years.
    </p>
    <p>
      The full year-by-year series for Concepción, including breakdown by crime family, is
      available on the <a href="/commune/concepcion/">Concepción commune detail page</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Concepción in the Biobío Region</h2>
    <p>
      Concepción anchors the Gran Concepción metropolitan area, which extends across several
      communes including Talcahuano (the port), San Pedro de la Paz (a southern residential
      zone), Chiguayante, Coronel, and others. Each has its own reported rate. The
      <a href="/region/biobio/">Biobío region page</a> provides a commune-by-commune ranking
      within the region.
    </p>
    <p>
      Concepción commune itself covers the commercial centre, the university district, and
      several neighbourhoods that vary in character. CEAD data does not resolve to
      neighbourhood level — all incidents are attributed to the commune as a whole.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>University City Dynamics</h2>
    <p>
      Concepción hosts several major universities, including Universidad de Concepción and
      Universidad del Bío-Bío, which together attract tens of thousands of students. A large
      student population typically increases both the resident count and the social activity
      level, affecting the types and patterns of incidents reported. Property crime — phone
      theft, bag snatching — tends to be the most common category in university areas
      according to CEAD data.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Crime Type Composition</h2>
    <p>
      Property crime (robbery and theft) represents the largest share of reported incidents
      in Concepción according to CEAD data, as in most Chilean urban communes. Intra-family
      violence is consistently the second most commonly reported category at national level.
      The crime-family breakdown for Concepción by year appears on the
      <a href="/commune/concepcion/">commune detail page</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Trends Over Time</h2>
    <p>
      Concepción's reported rate has been {trendLabel} in recent years according to CEAD data.
      The multi-year series from 2005 onward on the commune detail page provides context for
      interpreting the current rate — whether it represents a peak, a trough, or part of a
      longer directional movement. According to CEAD data for {year}, the commune's national
      rank was {concepcion.national_rank} out of all ranked communes.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Talcahuano and the Gran Concepción Area</h2>
    <p>
      Talcahuano, immediately northwest of Concepción, is Chile's principal naval base and a
      major industrial port commune. Its rate profile differs from Concepción's residential
      and commercial zones. Visitors arriving by sea or accessing the industrial area should
      note that Talcahuano is a separate commune with its own CEAD statistics.
    </p>
    <p>
      San Pedro de la Paz, connected to Concepción by the Bio-Bio bridge, is primarily a
      residential commune and typically shows a rate below Concepción commune's level — again,
      a separate commune with its own data page.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Practical Context for Visitors and Residents</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        The city centre and main bus terminal area see the highest concentration of reported
        property crime. Standard urban precautions apply.
      </li>
      <li>
        The university district around Barrio Universitario is lively at night and generally
        well-lit, though bag and phone theft are reported, as in any area with high
        pedestrian density.
      </li>
      <li>
        Residents evaluating neighbourhoods in the Gran Concepción area should compare
        commune-level pages for Concepción, Talcahuano, San Pedro de la Paz, and Chiguayante
        on this site for a data-grounded view.
      </li>
      <li>
        Connections to Chilean cities to the south (Temuco, Valdivia) are well served by bus
        and train from Concepción's terminal.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Data Source and Methodology</h2>
    <p>
      All statistics on this page come from CEAD (Centro de Estudios y Análisis del Delito),
      the official Chilean government crime statistics body, covering police-reported incidents
      for {year}. Rates are computed per 100,000 registered inhabitants. The national average
      is the unweighted mean across non-low-population communes. Full methodology is documented
      on the <a href="/methodology/">Methodology page</a>.
    </p>
  </section>
</EditorialLayout>

<style>
  section {
    margin-bottom: var(--2xl);
  }

  p + p {
    margin-top: var(--md);
  }

  ul {
    list-style: disc;
    padding-left: var(--lg);
    line-height: 1.8;
  }

  a {
    color: var(--primary);
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  a:hover {
    text-decoration: underline;
  }
</style>
